Here's a JavaScript function that takes a sentence as input and returns the same sentence with the order of adjectives switched:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i] === "beautiful" || words[i] === "green") {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse();
  const newSentence = "";

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (adjectives.includes(words[i])) {
      newSentence += reversedAdjectives[i] + " ";
    } else {
      newSentence += words[i] + " ";
    }
  }

  return newSentence;
}

const inputSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const outputSentence = switchAdjectives(inputSentence);
console.log(outputSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This code splits the input sentence into individual words, creates an array of adjectives, reverses the order of adjectives, and then builds a new sentence by alternating between adjectives and other words. Finally, it returns the new sentence.

