To blend different audio files and create a realistic experience of a summer day using Java programming language, you can use libraries such as SoX (Sound eXchange) or PortAudio. These libraries allow you to read and manipulate audio files, mix them together, and apply various effects.

Here's a simple example using SoX:
```java
import org.sox.Sox;
import org.sox.SoxException;

public class SummerDayAudio {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws SoxException {
        // Load the audio files
        Sox sox = new Sox();
        sox.addInput("birds.wav");
        sox.addInput("leaves.wav");
        sox.addInput("cicadas.wav");
        sox.addInput("children_playing.wav");

        // Mix the audio files
        sox.setOutput("summer_day.wav");
        sox.run();
    }
}
```
To enhance the overall experience, you can add artificial sounds such as laughter, splashing water, and ice clinking. You can also use effects such as reverb, echo, and equalization to create a more immersive experience.

To ensure that the audio narrative has a well-defined structure that directs the listeners through different scenarios, you can use a storyboard or a script. This will help you organize your thoughts and ensure that the audio narrative flows smoothly.

