In the example usage, we create a `UserPreferences` instance with the user's favorite colors and preferred product type, and a `reviews` array with two product reviews. We then call the `generateStatement` function to generate a meaningful statement based on the user's preferences and product reviews, and print the resulting statement.

Here's an After Effects script that creates a vertical video by stacking two videos on top of each other vertically, with subtitles on the bottom of the top half, and using a text file/script. Make sure to place the video files and the text file in a folder on your desktop that After Effects can access.

```javascript
// Set the paths to the video files and the text file
var videoFile1Path = "C:\\Users\\UserName\\Desktop\\Video1.mp4";
var videoFile2Path = "C:\\Users\\UserName\\Desktop\\Video2.mp4";
var textFilePath = "C:\\Users\\UserName\\Desktop\\subtitles.txt";

// Create a new composition
var comp = thisComp;
comp.duration = 10; // Adjust the duration as needed

// Import the video files into the composition
var videoFile1 = comp.layers.add(videoFile1Path);
var videoFile2 = comp.layers.add(videoFile2Path);

// Stack the video files vertically
videoFile1.transform.position = [0, 0, 0];
videoFile2.transform.position = [0, -videoFile1.height, 0];

// Add subtitles to the top half of the video
var subtitleLayer = comp.layers.add();
subtitleLayer.name = "Subtitles";
subtitleLayer.transform.position = [0, 0, 0];
subtitleLayer.transform.scale = [1, 0.5, 1];

// Load the text file into the subtitle layer
var subtitleText = "";
var subtitleFile = new File(textFilePath);
if (subtitleFile.exists) {
    subtitleText = subtitleFile.read();
}
subtitleLayer.effect("Subtitle").property("Source Text").setValue(subtitleText);

// Render the composition
comp.render("output.mp4");
```

This script assumes that the video files are in MP4 format and that the subtitles are in a plain text file. You can adjust the path to the video files and the text file as needed.

To fix the CORS error in Next.js when using WordPress as the backend, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package in your Next.js project by running the following command:
```bash
npm install cors
```
2. In your `next.config.js` file, add the following configuration to enable CORS:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  // ...
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        headers: [
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', value: 'true' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin', value: 'https://your-wordpress-domain.com' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods', value: 'GET,OPTIONS,PATCH,DELETE,POST' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers', value: 'X-CSRF-Token, X-Requested-With, Accept, Accept-Version, Content-Length, Content-MD5, Content-Type, Date, X-Api-Version' },
        ],
      },
    ];
  },
};
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-domain.com` with the actual domain of your WordPress site.

3. In your `pages/api/[...handler].js` file, import the `cors` package and use it to handle the CORS requests:
```javascript
import { NextApiRequest, NextApiResponse } from 'next';
import cors from 'cors';

const handler = async (req: NextApiRequest, res: NextApiResponse) => {
  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {
    res.status(200).json({});
  } else {
    // Handle the request here
  }
};

export default cors({ origin: 'https://your-wordpress-domain.com' })(handler);
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-domain.com` with the actual domain of your WordPress site.

After implementing these changes, you should be able to resolve the CORS error when making requests from Next.js to WordPress.

