To divide an hour into 10 equal parts, you can use the following formula in Excel:

`=TIME(HOUR(A1), (MINUTE(A1) / 6), 0)`

This formula will return the first 10-minute interval of the hour. You can then copy this formula down for the remaining intervals.

In MATLAB, you can use the `hour` and `minutes` functions to achieve the same result:

```matlab
% Assuming your data is stored in a column called 'time'
time = time(:); % Convert to cell array

% Extract the hour and minute components
hours = hour(time);
minutes = minutes(time);

% Calculate the 10-minute intervals
intervals = hours .* 60 + minutes;
intervals = intervals / 10;

% Round to the nearest minute
intervals = round(intervals);

% Convert back to time format
intervals = datetime(intervals, 'Format', 'hh:mm');
```

Both methods will give you 10 equally spaced intervals within each hour, allowing you to perform more granular analysis on your data.

Here's a Go program that parses a struct in a Go source file and generates a Avro schema from it. The generated schema will be saved in a file named `struct.avsc`.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"io/ioutil"
	"log"
	"reflect"
	"strings"

	"github.com/apache/avro/avro"
)

func main() {
	fileName := "example.go" // Replace with your Go source file name
	structName := "MyStruct"   // Replace with the name of the struct you want to convert

	content, err := ioutil.ReadFile(fileName)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	var structDef reflect.Struct
	err = reflect.New(reflect.TypeOf(structName)).Elem().AssignTo(&structDef)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	schema, err := avro.ConvertStructToSchema(structDef)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	err = ioutil.WriteFile("struct.avsc", []byte(schema), 0644)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	fmt.Println("Avro schema generated successfully and saved as struct.avsc")
}
```

Make sure to replace `example.go` with the name of your Go source file and `MyStruct` with the name of the struct you want to convert.

