Yes, you can achieve this using an SQL database by creating a trigger on the `work_sessions` table. A trigger is a special type of stored procedure that automatically executes when a specific event occurs, such as inserting, updating, or deleting data. In this case, we will use a trigger to check if 10 minutes have passed since the start_time of the current work session and prompt the user to take a break.

Here's an example of how you can create a trigger for this purpose:

```sql
CREATE TRIGGER take_break_trigger
AFTER UPDATE ON work_sessions
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN
    DECLARE start_time TIMESTAMP;
    DECLARE end_time TIMESTAMP;
    DECLARE current_time TIMESTAMP;

    SELECT start_time, end_time INTO start_time, end_time FROM work_sessions WHERE id = OLD.id;
    SET current_time = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP;

    IF (current_time - start_time) > INTERVAL 10 MINUTE THEN
        -- Prompt the user to take a break
        INSERT INTO notifications (message, created_at) VALUES ('Take a break!', current_time);
    END IF;
END;
```

This trigger will execute after an update on the `work_sessions` table, which could happen when the user starts or ends a work session. It will check if 10 minutes have passed since the start_time of the current work session and prompt the user to take a break if it has. The notification will be inserted into a separate table called `notifications`, which you can then use to display the break reminder message.

Note that this example assumes that the `start_time` and `end_time` columns in the `work_sessions` table are of type `TIMESTAMP`. If they are not, you may need to convert them to `TIMESTAMP` before comparing them.

Also, keep in mind that triggers can have performance implications, especially if they are executed frequently. Make sure to test your trigger carefully and monitor its performance to ensure that it does not impact the performance of your application.

To create a script for Unity that takes an audio file as input and displays a visualizer in time with the music, follow these steps:

1. Create a new C# script in Unity and name it "AudioVisualizer".
2. Add the following using statements at the top of the script:
```csharp
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.Audio;
using UnityEngine.UI;
```
1. Declare the necessary variables and objects:
```csharp
public AudioSource audioSource;
public GameObject visualizerObject;
public float visualizerSize = 10f;
public Color[] visualizerColors = new Color[10];
private float visualizerTime = 0f;
```
1. In the Start method, initialize the visualizer object and set its size and color array:
```csharp
void Start()
{
    visualizerObject.SetActive(false);
    visualizerObject.transform.localScale = Vector3.one * visualizerSize;
    visualizerObject.GetComponent<Renderer>().material.color = visualizerColors[0];
}
```
1. In the Update method, update the visualizer based on the audio source's position:
```csharp
void Update()
{
    if (audioSource.isPlaying)
    {
        visualizerTime += Time.deltaTime;
        float progress = audioSource.time / audioSource.clip.length;
        float angle = progress * 360f;
        visualizerObject.transform.rotation = Quaternion.Euler(0f, 0f, angle);
