Natural selection can be modeled using MATLAB. Here's a simple example:

```MATLAB
% Define population parameters
population_size = 100;
mutation_rate = 0.01;
selection_pressure = 0.5;

% Initialize population
population = randi([0 1], population_size, 1);

% Simulate evolution over time
for i = 1:100
    % Compute fitness based on selection pressure
    fitness = population .* selection_pressure;

    % Apply mutation
    mutated_population = population .+ mutation_rate .* randn(population_size, 1);

    % Select new population based on fitness
    new_population = mutated_population(fitness > rand(population_size));

    % Update population for next iteration
    population = new_population;
end

% Plot the evolution of the population
figure;
plot(1:100, mean(population));
xlabel('Generation');
ylabel('Fitness');
title('Evolution under Natural Selection');
```

This code simulates the evolution of a population under natural selection. The population is initialized with random values, and then evolves over time through selection and mutation. The fitness of each individual is computed based on the selection pressure, and then used to select the new population. The mutation rate introduces new genetic variations into the population, which can lead to the emergence of new traits that are better adapted to the environment. Over time, the population evolves to become more fit, as measured by the average fitness of the individuals.

