Here's a simple C++ program representing a book with member variables for the title, author, and summary:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Book {
public:
    std::string title;
    std::string author;
    std::string summary;

    void setTitle(const std::string& t) {
        title = t;
    }

    void setAuthor(const std::string& a) {
        author = a;
    }

    void setSummary(const std::string& s) {
        summary = s;
    }

    std::string getTitle() const {
        return title;
    }

    std::string getAuthor() const {
        return author;
    }

    std::string getSummary() const {
        return summary;
    }

    bool isEqual(const Book& other) const {
        return (title == other.title && author == other.author && summary == other.summary);
    }
};

int main() {
    Book catcherInTheRye("The Catcher in the Rye", "J.D. Salinger", "A coming-of-age novel about a troubled teenager.");
    Book anotherBook("To Kill a Mockingbird", "Harper Lee", "A classic novel about racial injustice and moral courage.");
    Book thirdBook("1984", "George Orwell", "A dystopian novel about a totalitarian society where individuality is suppressed.");

    std::cout << "Is 'The Catcher in the Rye' equal to 'To Kill a Mockingbird'?" << std::endl;
    std::cout << catcherInTheRye.isEqual(anotherBook) << std::endl; // Output: false

    std::cout << "Is 'The Catcher in the Rye' equal to '1984'?" << std::endl;
    std::cout << catcherInTheRye.isEqual(thirdBook) << std::endl; // Output: false

    return 0;
}
```

This program defines a `Book` class with member variables for the title, author, and summary

To configure the boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage in your Chrome extension, you can use the `chrome.tabs` API to get the tab ID and then use `chrome.tabs.executeScript` to inject the JavaScript code. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. First, create a background script (e.g., `background.js`) for your extension. This script will handle the injection of JavaScript code into the webpage.
2. In the `background.js` script, add the following code to get the current tab ID and inject the JavaScript code:
```javascript
chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, tabs => {
  const tabId = tabs[0].id;
  chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabId, {file: 'inject.js'});
});
```
This code uses the `chrome.tabs.query` method to get the current tab ID and then uses `chrome.tabs.executeScript` to inject the JavaScript code from the `inject.js` file.
3. Create a new file called `inject.js` in the same directory as your `background.js` script. This file will contain the JavaScript code that you want to inject into the webpage.
4. In the `inject.js` file, add the JavaScript code that you want to inject into the webpage. For example:
```javascript
console.log('Hello, world!');
```
This code will log "Hello, world!" to the console when the JavaScript code is executed.
5. Now, you can pack your extension by running `chrome://extensions/` in your Chrome browser and selecting "Pack Extension" from the menu. This will create a .crx file that you can distribute as your extension.
6. To install the extension, go to `chrome://extensions/` again and click "Load unpacked" to select the .crx file you just created.
7. Once the extension is installed, it will automatically inject the JavaScript code from the `inject.js` file into any webpage you visit.

That's it! With these steps, you should be able to configure the boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage in your Chrome extension.

Here's a sample code using TypeScript, React, and Downshift to create an email input field with autocomplete functionality:

```typescript
import { useCombobox } from 'downshift';
import axios from 'axios';

interface Email {
  email: string;
}

const EmailAutocomplete: React.FC = () => {
  const [emails, setEmails] = React.useState<Email[]>([]);
  const {
    isOpen,
    selectedItem,
    getMenuProps,
    getInputProps,
    getComboboxProps,
    highlightedIndex,
  } = useCombobox({
    items: emails,
  });

  const searchEmails = async (prefix: string) => {
    try {
      const response = await axios.get(`https://api.example.com/emails`, {
        params: { prefix },
      });
      setEmails(response.data);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
    }
  };

  const handleInputChange = (event: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
    searchEmails(event.target.value);
  };

  return (
    <div {...getComboboxProps()}>
      <input
        {...getInputProps({
          type: 'text',
          placeholder: 'Search emails',
          onChange: handleInputChange,
        })}
      />
      {isOpen && (
        <ul {...getMenuProps()}>
          {emails.map((email, index) => (
            <li
              key={index}
              {...getMenuItemProps({
                index,
                item: email,
                isSelected: selectedItem === email,
                style: {
                  backgroundColor: highlightedIndex === index ? '#f2f2f2' : 'white',
                },
              })}
            >
              {email.email}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

export default EmailAutocomplete;
```

